{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And a man who becomes impure and does not purify himself.</b> Why say this? Was it not already stated, 'and does not purify himself — the Mishkan of HaShem has he defiled'? Rather, so that you not say: for one who touches, the verse was stringent — if he entered the Sanctuary without sprinkling; but for one who defiles a tent, if the water was not sprinkled upon him and he entered the Sanctuary, he is not liable to karet — therefore it says <b>and a man who becomes impure</b> by the matters stated above. <b>And does not purify himself and shall be cut off</b> etc. — the punishment we have heard; whence the warning? It says, 'and they shall send out of the camp every leper and every zav and everyone impure through a corpse.'" "<b>For the sanctuary of HaShem has he defiled.</b> That he entered it while still impure. And if it says Mishkan, why does it say Mikdash, and if it says Mikdash, why does it say Mishkan? Rather: if it said Mishkan and not Mikdash, I might say: for the Mishkan he is liable, since it was anointed with the anointing oil, but for the Mikdash he is not liable — therefore it says Mikdash. And if it said Mikdash and not Mishkan, I might say: for the Mikdash he is liable, since its sanctity is eternal sanctity, but for the Mishkan he is not liable — therefore it says both Mishkan and Mikdash."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:47.881535379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}